    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kamehameha rose from guardian of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku in 1782 to king of Hawaiʻi island in 1791, with the backing of five Kona chiefs and guns bought from British and American traders.</li><li>In 1795 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> with an armada of 960 war canoes and 10,000 soldiers, and in April 1810 he brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> into the kingdom by negotiation with its king, Kaumualiʻi.</li><li>As ruler he unified the legal system and used the products collected in taxes to promote trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, so that the islands would remain one realm after his death.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After Kamehameha died in 1819 his friends Hoapili and Hoʻolulu hid his body according to the custom called hūnākele, because of the sacred power, or mana, of a person. His final resting place remains unknown.</p></aside>
    </section>

