    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1736</span><span class="rf-text">Kamehameha is born, by most accounts in Ainakea, Kohala; sources place the birth between 1736 and 1761</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1782</span><span class="rf-text">On the death of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> he is made guardian of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku and given the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waipio-valley">Waipiʻo Valley</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1790</span><span class="rf-text">He takes possession of the ship Fair American and, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> at the Battle of Kepaniwai</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1791</span><span class="rf-text">His rival Keōua Kūʻahuʻula is killed on landing at the newly completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pu-ukohola-heiau-national-historic-site">Puʻukoholā Heiau</a>, and Kamehameha becomes King of Hawaiʻi island</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1795</span><span class="rf-text">He secures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> and defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> at Nuʻuanu, where over 700 men are forced over the Pali's cliff</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1796</span><span class="rf-text">A first attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> fails when many of his canoes are lost in the Kaʻieʻie Waho channel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1810</span><span class="rf-text">He negotiates the peaceful unification of the islands with Kaumualiʻi, who remains tributary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">Kamehameha dies on May 8 or 14, and his body is buried in a hidden place</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kamehameha, known as Paiʻea at birth, was the son of Kekuʻiapoiwa II, niece of Alapainui, the usurping ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> Island, who had killed the two legitimate heirs of Keaweʻīkekahialiʻiokamoku during civil war. By most accounts he was born in Ainakea, Kohala. His father was Keōua Kalanikupuapa'ikalaninui. The Native Hawaiian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-kamakau">Samuel Kamakau</a> says that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> monarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> adopted him at birth in the traditional, informal way called hānai, and that this is why Kahekili is often called his father; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> dismissed such rumours as scandals born of the hatred and jealousies of later years. Keōua acknowledged him as his son, and he was in any case a descendant of Keawe through his mother.</p><p>Accounts of his birth vary, and sources place it between 1736 and 1761. Kamakau, writing in 1867, put it around 1736, during the warfare among the chiefs that followed the death of Keawe. His dating has been challenged as twenty years too early, but it was widely accepted because of the support of Abraham Fornander, who reckoned that Kamehameha was past eighty when he died. The historian Ralph Simpson Kuykendall believed the birth fell between 1748 and 1761. A date of 1758, linked to a visit of Halley's Comet, does not accord with accounts of his fighting beside his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> and would place his birth after his father's death.</p><p>At the time of his birth Keōua and his half-brother Kalaniʻōpuʻu were serving Alapaʻinui. Keōua died while Kamehameha was very young, and the boy was raised in the court of his uncle Kalaniʻōpuʻu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kamehameha achieved prominence in 1782, on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>. The kingship passed to Kalaniʻōpuʻu's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kīwalaʻō</a>, while Kamehameha was given a prominent religious position as guardian of the Hawaiian god of war, Kūkaʻilimoku, and control of the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waipio-valley">Waipiʻo Valley</a>. Relations between the cousins were strained after Kamehameha made a dedication to the gods instead of allowing Kīwalaʻō to do so. A further story tells that when Kamehameha was able to lift the Naha Stone he was held to be the fulfiller of a prophecy, which other ruling chiefs rejected.</p><p>He accepted the allegiance of five chiefs of the Kona district: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ke-eaumoku-papa-iahiahi">Keʻeaumoku Pāpaʻiahiahi</a>, Keaweaheulu Kaluaʻāpana, Kekūhaupiʻo, his teacher in war, and the twins Kameʻeiamoku and Kamanawa. Kīwalaʻō was soon defeated in the first key conflict, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Battle of Mokuʻōhai</a>, and Kamehameha and his chiefs took over the districts of Kohala, Kona and Hāmākua on Hawaiʻi. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a>, daughter of Keʻeaumoku, became his senior counsellor and one of Hawaiʻi's most powerful figures.</p><p>Foreign arms helped him. British and American traders sold him guns and ammunition, and Captain William Brown of the Butterworth Squadron guaranteed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> and gave him its formula. In 1790 the chief Kameʻeiamoku seized the ship Fair American and killed all but one of its crew; Kamehameha took the survivor, Isaac Davis, into protection and took possession of the ship. Davis and John Young, boatswain of the Eleanora, were treated as chiefs, given wives and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, and became his advisers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1790, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> was on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, Kamehameha's army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> with the help of Young and Davis and, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> from the Fair American, defeated Kahekili's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> at the bloody Battle of Kepaniwai. Kahekili reconquered Maui in 1791 and tried to invade Hawaiʻi, but was defeated in a naval battle called Kepuwahaʻulaʻula near Waipiʻo. With the death of Keōua Kūʻahuʻula in 1791, Kamehameha became King of Hawaiʻi island.</p><p>Kahekili died in 1794, and a civil war followed between his son Kalanikūpule and his brother Kāʻeokūlani. Seeing an opportunity, Kamehameha set sail in 1795 with an armada of 960 war canoes and 10,000 soldiers. He quickly secured the lightly defended islands of Maui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> at the Battle of Kawela and landed on Oʻahu at Waiʻalae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikiki">Waikīkī</a>. At Nuʻuanu his troops took heavy fire from cannon until two divisions climbed the Pali and seized the guns from behind. His forces killed most of Kalanikūpule's army, and over 700 men were forced over the Pali's cliff, a drop of 1,000 feet.</p><p>An attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> in the spring of 1796 failed when many canoes were lost in the Kaʻieʻie Waho channel. In April 1810 he negotiated the peaceful unification of the islands with Kaumualiʻi, who remained tributary king of Kauaʻi. As ruler he unified the legal system and used the products collected in taxes to promote trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He also declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanawai-mamalahoe">Law of the Splintered Paddle</a>: "Let every elderly person, woman, and child lie by the roadside in safety".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kamehameha's power was won against other chiefs, and several of them died at his hands or those of his followers. His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kīwalaʻō</a>, the heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>, was defeated at Mokuʻōhai. Keōua Kūʻahuʻula, who ruled Kaʻū and Puna, raided the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> while Kamehameha was on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1790 and deposed his own uncle Keawemaʻuhili in Hilo. In 1791 Kamehameha invited him to the newly completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pu-ukohola-heiau-national-historic-site">Puʻukoholā Heiau</a>. As Keōua stepped ashore one of Kamehameha's chiefs threw a spear at him; by some accounts he dodged it and was cut down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire.</p><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> in 1795 a high-ranking commander, Kaʻiana, defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> and was killed in the fighting; Kalanikūpule was later captured and sacrificed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ku-hawaiian-god">Kūkāʻilimoku</a>. Kaʻiana's brother Nāmakehā then rebelled in Hilo, and Kamehameha returned to Hawaii to put the rebellion down. In 1810 the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> of his council wished to kill Kaumualiʻi, a course Kamehameha was reluctant to take; only his high priest and genealogist Kalaikuʻahulu agreed with him. The chiefs went on with a plan to poison Kaumualiʻi, and when Isaac Davis warned him, Davis was poisoned instead.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanawai-mamalahoe">Law of the Splintered Paddle</a> itself began with an attack on innocent people. During a raid in 1782 Kamehameha caught his foot in a rock, and two fishermen struck him on the head with a paddle and left him for dead. When they were brought before him twelve years later he blamed himself, gave them land and set them free. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-kamakau">Kamakau</a>'s chronology of his life has also been disputed, as inaccurate when set against the accounts of foreign visitors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, Kamehameha ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> for six years while he consolidated his conquests and prepared a second invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a>. At Hilo he commissioned a fleet of double-hulled war canoes called peleleu, 800 according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-kamakau">Kamakau</a>, along with Western schooners, and stockpiled guns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and ammunition. He took the fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, where he stayed from 1802 to 1803, and to Oʻahu in late 1803 or early 1804. There a large percentage of his force was killed by the maʻi ʻokuʻu epidemic, thought to have been either cholera or bubonic plague. Kamehameha contracted the illness but survived, and the invasion was postponed; the settlement with Kaumualiʻi followed in April 1810.</p><p>He remained the holder of the war god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ku-hawaiian-god">Kūkāʻilimoku</a>. The explorer George Vancouver noted that he worshipped his gods and wooden images in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiau</a>. Kamehameha told Vancouver that the gods he worshipped were gods with mana, through whom he had become supreme ruler of all the islands, and Vancouver decided against sending missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>After about 1812 Kamehameha returned from Oahu and spent the last years of his life at Kamakahonu, a compound he built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailua-kona">Kailua-Kona</a>. As was the custom of the time he had several wives and many children, though he outlived many of them. He died on May 8 or 14, 1819. His trusted friends Hoapili and Hoʻolulu hid his body according to the ancient custom called hūnākele, and his resting place remains unknown. Only his children by his highest-ranking wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a>, succeeded him on the throne.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kamehameha is remembered as the conqueror and first ruler of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, and he took steps to ensure that the islands remained a united realm after his death. According to Malcolm Naea Chun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a>'s ending of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">Kapu</a> system as the best way to ensure that Kamehameha's children and grandchildren would rule the kingdom. The first company of the American Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions arrived on March 30, 1820, less than a year after he died. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> later asked Hoapili to show him where his father's bones lay, but Hoapili, knowing they were being followed, turned back.</p><p>The size of his family is debated, because documents recording the names of his wives were destroyed. Hiram Bingham I lists 21 wives and Mary Kawena Pukui counted 26; the authors of Kamehameha's Children Today list 30 wives and 35 children, 17 sons and 18 daughters.</p><p>He is honoured in the Hawaiian public holiday King Kamehameha I Day, first proclaimed in 1871 by his grandson King Kamehameha V. The state of Hawaii gave a statue of him to the National Statuary Hall Collection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, as one of the two it is entitled to place there. A Benjamin Franklin-class ballistic missile submarine, the USS Kamehameha, was named after him, and he appears on the quarter issued in 2008 for Hawaiʻi in the 50 State quarters programme. His name has also travelled into popular culture: Akira Toriyama took it for a signature move in the series Dragon Ball, and the Apple TV series Chief of War depicts the unification of the islands under him.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kamehameha I rose from a chief of Hawaiʻi Island to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> by 1795, with the help of Western guns and advisers, and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> into his realm by negotiation in 1810. The first ruler of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, he unified its legal system and promoted trade before his death in 1819.</em></p>

